questions.explanation:
The 'override' keyword in the Dog class replaces the base class's Speak method. When dog.Speak() is called, the overridden version runs, printing 'Dog Speak'. Without 'override' (using 'new' instead), it would also print 'Dog Speak' when called on a Dog reference.

public class Animal { public virtual void Speak() { Console.WriteLine("Animal Speak"); } }
public class Dog : Animal { public override void Speak() { Console.WriteLine("Dog Speak"); } }
var dog = new Dog(); dog.Speak();

